> varP3 is available to estimate the encounter rate variance for point
> transects in the 'mrds' R package.
>
> Is there a reason for this?
>
> Our data arise from a camera trap distance sampling survey. The design
> was systematic (a grid). In spite of this, after reading Fewster et
> al.'s article and the Web Appendix, I was planning to use varP2 with our
> data, because the number of "visits" to each point varies, and it does
> not seem reasonable to assume that the encounter rate is similar
> throughout the region. Also, from the manual for Program Distance:
> "Fewster et al. (2009) found that R2 slightly out-performed R3 in

> simulations, and for this reason _as well as because no assumptions are
> made about animal distribution_, this estimator is the default in

> Distance.... For point transects, the equivalent estimator to R2 is P2."
